Let f(x, y) be a scalar-valued function of two variables. 
In 2.3, we understood the partial derivative (δf)/(δx) (a, b) as the slope, at the point (a, b, f(a, b)), of the curve obtained as the intersection of the surface z = f(x, y) with the plane y = b. 
The other partial derivative (δf)/(δy) (a, b) has a similar geometric interpretation. 
However, the surface z = f(x, y) contains infinitely many curves passing through (a, b, f(a, b)) whose slope we might choose to measure. 
THe directional derivative enables use to do this

